#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mkrows15.py > rows : the round 15 ledger rows (eight cells each, no bare pipe inside a cell)."""
T = 'Docs/Decompiler/tools/a3_round15'
rows = []
def row(id_, sev, finding, fixture, status='open'):
    cells = [id_, '3', sev, finding, fixture, status, 'area 3 round 15 review', '']
    for c in cells:
        assert '|' not in c.replace('\\|', ''), (id_, c)
        assert '\n' not in c
    rows.append('| ' + ' | '.join(cells) + ' |')

row('A3-130', 'P1',
 "A STORE through a pointer reached by two or more frame slots is lost while the frame has not escaped (A3-124's class, one pointer deeper; 0068 does not reach it). `S` at frame+12, slot1 (frame+32) holds &S, slot2 (frame+36) holds &slot1; the store is `mov.l @(36,r15),r1 ; mov.l @r1,r1 ; add #10,r1 ; mov.w r2,@r1` (the pointer is a LOAD through a stack read). S+8 is zeroed, the halfword -45 is stored at S+10, S+8 is read as a word: the code returns 0xFFD3; the text is lent `int4 f(void)` and `return 0;` (the word store of -1 at S+8, R15-7d: `return 0;` for 0xFFFFFFD3; three slots, R15-7c: the same). Mechanism, traced with a temporary build: heritage pass 1 guards the STORE (it is marked, being a LOAD of a stack word) on S, both slots and PR; `Heritage::reprocessFreeStores` runs in the same pass, with the stack linked the first reload is a modeled frame address, `FrameEscape::compute` has `case CPUI_LOAD: continue` (what a LOAD reads is memory), the STORE's pointer is no member, the STORE is unmarked and its four INDIRECT guards are destroyed (`opDestroy` from `reprocessFreeStores`); the direct read of S+8 is linked to the older write, the LOAD through &slot1 folds a pass later into a stack read, the STORE after it into a COPY nothing reads, and dead code removes it. 0068's delay raises only for a read found late: the late item here is a write, so one 'Restarted to delay deadcode elimination' warning is printed and the store is still lost. A call handed a frame address (R15-8a, 8c), the frame address published in a global (8b), a read through the chain (8d) or one slot (R15-7b) are right, because the frame escapes or the guard is not needed. Wrong on f3c13b0 as on ec93ad3. The frame ledger sees it (`LEDGER BAD`: the frame write of 2 bytes went unread, read at the word read); the printed-C differential sees it (`mismatch`, run 0000FFD3 text 00000000). frame124 draws one slot only; `frame15.py` with two or three levels and no fill: 6 to 10 of 60 fixtures of seed 5 per configuration have `LEDGER BAD`.",
 "`%s/r15h.src` R15-7a, 7c, 7d, `r15s.src` R15-18a (the -O0 form, r14 the frame pointer: `return 0;` too) (controls 7b, `r15i.src` 8a to 8d); R15-7a 06009000: `4F22 7FD0 61F3 710C 1F18 61F3 7120 1F19 E300 1F35 E2D3 51F9 6112 710A 2121 50F5 7F30 4F26 000B 0009` (sts.l pr ; add #-48,r15 ; S=sp+12 ; slot1=&S ; slot2=&slot1 ; S+8=0 ; r2=-45 ; two reloads ; halfword at S+10 ; read S+8). Replay: `SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS=\"4F22 7FD0 61F3 710C 1F18 61F3 7120 1F19 E300 1F35 E2D3 51F9 6112 710A 2121 50F5 7F30 4F26 000B 0009\"`. Wrong: lent, `return 0;`. Right: `return 0xffd3;` (what the one-slot control R15-7b prints). Oracle: the run line and the frame ledger." % T)

row('A3-131', 'P1',
 "Regression of patch 0068 (A3-127's front piece, exposed, f3c13b0 is right): a word that is built from stores through a pointer reloaded from a frame slot over bytes the function never wrote, then returned, is returned as its low piece. `S+4` (uninitialised) gets the halfword r5 at +2 and the halfword r6 at +0 through the reloaded pointer, the word is read directly and returned: the code returns 0x66664444 (r6 low, r5 low); lent `int4 f(int, int, int)` and `return param_2;` (R15-12a; bytes R15-12b, R15-13d `return param_2;` for the four byte stores r5 at +3, r6 at +2, r7 at +1, r4 at +0, code 0x22886644; halfwords of one register R15-12d). The base prints `xStack_24 = CONCAT22(param_3,param_2); return xStack_24;`. With the front piece set back to upstream's `bytesAfter` the head is right again (local build), so the cause is 0068's front length in `opCollapseIndirectsForCopy`: the collapse now builds `SUB42(in_stack,2)` for the bytes before the first COPY, a free stack input (the word was never written), and the data-flow that reaches the return carries it (pass 3: `CONCAT22(r6.lo,SUB42(CONCAT22(SUB42(in,2),r5.lo),0))`); at pass 4 the return is `r0+2 = r5+2 ; return r0+2`, the low two bytes only (printed with `fd->printRaw` in a temporary build). By reading, not instrumented: the return recovery (`AncestorRealistic`) takes the high half, whose chain ends in a free stack input, for unreal data flow and shrinks the return to its low piece. The same word stored to a global, passed to a lent callee or added to a parameter is right (R15-14a to 14c); a zeroed word first (R15-12c), direct stores (R15-13a), a pointer in a register (R15-13c) or a call handed &S after the stores (the frame escapes, R15-16a) are right. The -O0 form with r14 as the frame pointer (R15-16b) is wrong the same way (`return param_2;`, base `return xStack_28;`). The frame ledger says ok (no access is lost, no piece is malformed: the pieces add up); the printed-C differential finds it (`mismatch`, `size=2 upper-bits` in the generator's own words: `F15.31.297`, seed 31 of `frame15.py`: 1 of 700 call-free L1 fixtures). The generator's own frame124 stream draws it in the printed-C range 3,902,001 to 3,904,000 (`frame124_3902022`, `mismatch`, `size=1 upper-bits`: the code returns 0x6688FA22 for r4=11112222, the text is `xunknown1 func_0x06009000(xunknown1 param_1) { return param_1; }`, a one-byte return; the base prints `return xStack_18;` with a four-byte return), so the nightly range 1 to 20,000 does not reach it. A real shape: a struct of two shorts or four chars built through a local pointer and returned as a word.",
 "`%s/r15m.src` R15-12a to 12e, `r15n.src` R15-13d, `r15q.src` R15-16b (controls 12c, 13a, 13c, `r15o.src` 14a to 14c, `r15q.src` 16a); R15-12a 06009000 with args 11112222 33334444 55556666 77778888: `4F22 7FD0 61F3 710C 1F18 51F8 7106 2151 51F8 7104 2161 E000 61F3 710C 7104 6312 203A 7F30 4F26 000B 0009 0009` (lent from r4 to r7). Wrong: lent, `return param_2;` (r0=33334444 as text; the code returns 66664444). Right: `xStack_24 = CONCAT22(param_3,param_2); return xStack_24;` (the base's and the zeroed control's). Second source: `SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS=\"2FE6 7FE0 6EF3 61E3 7108 1E17 51E7 6313 7301 2340 51E7 6313 7307 2350 51E7 6313 7302 2360 51E7 6313 7306 2360 51E7 6313 2360 51E7 E219 6313 7303 2320 51E7 6313 7304 2370 51E7 6313 7305 2370 51E7 6313 2370 51E7 6313 7307 2340 51E7 E230 6313 7303 2320 51E7 E2FA 6313 7306 2320 51E7 6313 7303 2340 51E7 6313 7303 2370 51E7 6313 7304 2360 51E7 6313 7301 2370 63E3 730C 6232 6023 7E20 6FE3 000B 6EF6\"` (the same words as a driver spec: `g3902022.spec`). Oracle: the run line; printed-C replay of R15-10a (`r15k.src`, the shrunk seed-31 case): `SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS=\"4F22 7FD0 61F3 710C 1F18 51F8 7106 2121 51F8 7104 2121 51F8 7108 2152 E000 61F3 710C 7104 6312 203A 7F30 4F26 000B 0009\"`." % T)

row('A3-132', 'P1',
 "The entry value of a callee-saved register stored into a frame word (any store: `mov.l r8,@(8,r15)`, a push, via r10; r8 to r14, GBR and VBR, not MACL or a scratch input) and then overwritten through a pointer reloaded from a frame slot is read back as the register: `return unaff_r8;` where the code returns r5. `add #-16,r15 ; mov.l r8,@(8,r15) ; mov r15,r1 ; add #8,r1 ; mov.l r1,@r15 ; mov.l @r15,r2 ; mov.l r5,@r2 ; mov.l @(8,r15),r0` (no call): the signature is withheld ('it reads r8 on entry') and the text is `return unaff_r8;`; byte and halfword overwrites through the reload too (R15-6c, 6d), a halfword or byte store of r8 is data and is right (R15-5i, 5j), the pointer in a register (R15-6b) or published in a global first (R15-6a) is right. Trace: the guards of the STORE are made on all three ranges at pass 1 and `opCollapseIndirectsForAlias` destroys the one on the save slot at once (`noalias=1`, `guard=0`): `ActionRestrictLocal` marks the storage of a COPY of an unaffected register as not mapped (`markNotMapped`, the save slot), 0063's exception for a slot an indexed STORE may write (`isGuarded` of a valid STORE guard) does not hold a pointer reloaded from a frame slot, so the slot has no local alias, the guard goes, the store folds later into a COPY nothing reads. Wrong on f3c13b0 and ec93ad3 alike (the A3-124 class through the save-slot mechanism of A3-102; patch 0068's save exemption (`isSavePush`) is not involved: pushes and plain stores behave the same). The frame ledger finds it (`LEDGER BAD`, 6 of 11 variants). In compiled code the save slot is never overwritten; a local that starts as a callee-saved register's entry value (a function that uses r8 before setting it) is the same word. 5 of 21 one-slot and 4 of 23 two-slot `fill=save` fixtures of `frame15.py` seed 1 have `LEDGER BAD`.",
 "`%s/r15f.src` R15-5a to 5k, `r15e.src` R15-4a, `r15g.src` R15-6a to 6d, `r15s.src` R15-18b (the -O0 form, r14 the frame pointer: `return unaff_r8;` too); R15-5a 06009000 args 0 0 0 0 r8=11 and 1 2 3 4 r8=66: `4F22 7FF0 1F82 61F3 7108 2F12 62F2 2252 50F2 7F10 4F26 000B 0009 0009`. Wrong: withheld, `return unaff_r8;`. Right: `return param_2;` (R15-5d, the same with r3 for r8, lent). Oracle: the run line (r0 = r5) and the frame ledger." % T)

row('A3-133', 'P2',
 "Instrument: the frame ledger's own caps fail open. `Funcdata::noteFrameFoldForwarded` walks back from a forwarded value through at most 64 ops, then sets `frameFoldsAllRead` (and 4,096 guard records do the same) and the function's every frame write is taken as read: a function whose loss is hidden by a 64-op expression is reported clean. R15-15a (A3-130's shape) is `LEDGER BAD`; the same function with 10 xor/add operations on the result is `LEDGER BAD`; with 40 (R15-15c) or 80 (R15-15d) it is `LEDGER ok` while the text is the same wrong text (`return ((param_2 * 2 + ...` without the 0xffd3 term; printed-C `mismatch`, run 000113D3 text 00001400). Counted with a temporary print where the walk hits the cap (`dbg_engine.diff`, `SE_DBG_CAP=1`): 90 of the 839 functions of hwram007 and 228 of the 1,554 of hwram008 (11% and 15%) are not checked for lost frame writes at all; the round's '0 frame writes lost' holds for the rest. In `frame15.py` seed 61 (two slots, 250 fixtures, call-free) the printed-C differential finds 107 wrong texts and the ledger 101 `BAD`: 17 wrong texts are `LEDGER ok` (the large ones, 264 body bytes and more; the cap is the likely cause, not instrumented per fixture), 11 `BAD` fixtures have the right value (a lost store nothing returns). A3-129's text calls this 'may miss a loss, never report one that is none' and counts on the generator; a cap that fails open is what round 14's A3-122 was.",
 "`%s/r15p.src` R15-15a to 15d (`NOLEDGER` unset: the driver prints `LEDGER BAD`/`ok`); `dbg_engine.diff` for the count (not applied; `SE_DBG_CAP=1 SE_GAME_HWRAM=<img> SE_GAME_LEND=1 SaturnExplorerSh2DecompilerTests <dir> 2> err ; grep FRAMECAP err ; sort -u`). Wrong: ledger ok on R15-15c and 15d. Right: BAD, as on 15a and 15b (the cap should keep the function unchecked and say so, or fail the run; a taken-as-read answer should count as 'unchecked' in the corpus line)." % T)

row('A3-134', 'P3',
 "Instrument: the generator draws none of A3-130 to A3-132 and the frame ledger cannot see A3-131. `GenFrame124Fn` has one pointer slot, always fills S whole first, and never stores a callee-saved register's entry value into S; `frame15.py` (two or three slots, no fill, `fill=save`) finds all three at once: 31 of 200 fixtures of seed 1 have `LEDGER BAD`. A3-131 is a value error with no lost access, so only the printed-C differential can see it, and it sees call-free functions only.",
 "`%s/frame15.py` (`python3 frame15.py <seed> <count>`, knobs `F15_LEVELS`, `F15_NST`, `F15_CALL`, `F15_FILL`), `f15run.sh`, `f15sum.py`, `f15pc.py`, `shrink15.py`; `python3 frame15.py 1 200` with `F15_FILL` unset (31 of 200 `LEDGER BAD`), `F15_LEVELS=2 F15_NST=3 F15_FILL=none python3 frame15.py 5 60` (10 of 60). Right: a generator stream with the three shapes, folded into `GenFrame124Fn` (slots chained, S not filled first, `fill=save`, S tiled by stores)." % T)

row('A3-135', 'P3',
 "A lent prototype that drops r8 when the function hands the address of r8's push slot to a callee that reads it: `mov.l r8,@-r15 ; sts.l pr,@-r15 ; mov r15,r4 ; add #4,r4 ; jsr g ; ... ` with `g: mov.l @r4,r0 ; rts ; nop` is lent as `xunknown4 f(void)` and returns g's value, the caller's r8 (R15-1a); the frame address published in a global and read by g (R15-1b) is the same. A callee-saved register's push is no read, by 0068's `isSavePush` and, before it, by the lending trials; here the slot is read as data by a callee and the lent signature misses an input. Wrong on f3c13b0 as well (the trials treat a push as a save), so not a regression; the A3-124 fix's record lists it as a limit ('none of the fixtures, generator seeds or corpus functions read here does'). Not found in either save state. P3 for that and because it needs a function that gives away its own save area.",
 "`%s/r15a.src` R15-1a, 1b (also 1c: the push as the fifth stack argument of an unlent callee, withheld and right); R15-1a 06009000, 06009100 lent, args 0 0 0 0 r8=12345678: `2F86 4F22 64F3 7404 D103 410B 0009 4F26 7F04 000B 0009 0009 0600 9100`. Wrong: lent, `xVar1 = func_0x06009100(axStack_208 + 0x81)` with no parameter. Right: withheld ('it reads r8 on entry'), or a parameter for r8. Status: P3, to accept with a reason or fix with A3-132." % T, status='open')

row('A3-136', 'P3',
 "Frame ledger false loss (oracle, A3-129's family, found by the generator in grammar 2 seed 3901693): a frame write is reported lost because of a call the engine removed as unreachable. `mov #1,r3 ; dt r3 ; mov.b r3,@r14 ; ... ; tst r3,r3 ; bt L ; bsr g ; nop ; L:` stores r3 (0) at the frame word; r3 is 0, `bt` is always taken, the engine prints `Removing unreachable block (ram,0x06009032)` and no call. The code reads the byte nowhere (the trace has no read of 0603FFB8), the text is right (the loop and the array only); but `Heritage::guardCalls` noted the `bsr` as a call guarded at the local (`Funcdata::noteFrameCall`), the call went with its block, and the record stayed, so the ledger finds 'the frame write of 1 byte(s) at 0600901E (frame offset FFFFFFB8) went unread, and the read at 00000000 reads it'. `dropFrameCall` handles a guard `RuleAliasUpdate` removes, not a call removed with an unreachable block; `fate_frame_removed` is for accesses. The generator fails (`n:frame-lost:W1`, one finding in 5,000 seeds of 3,900,001 to 3,905,000), so the nightly would be red on a longer range. No wrong text.",
 "`SE_GENERATED_GRAMMAR=2 SE_GENERATED_FIRST=3901693 SE_GENERATED_SEEDS=1 SaturnExplorerSh2GeneratedTests <dir>`; the shrunk replay: `SE_GENERATED_WORDS=\"2F86 2F96 2FA6 2FB6 2FE6 4F22 7FD0 6EF3 6843 6953 6A63 6B73 E301 4310 8BFD 2E30 62F3 7230 E103 2216 4110 8BFC E200 2338 8905 B00E 0009 6483 6593 66A3 67B3 7E30 6FE3 4F26 6EF6 6BF6 6AF6 69F6 68F6 000B 0009 7FE0 6043 7F20 000B 0009\" SE_GENERATED_FUNCS=\"0/0/52,52/52/5c\" SE_GENERATED_RETURNS=\"0,1\" SE_GENERATED_CLOBBERS=\"0,0\" SE_GENERATED_READS=\"0,20000\"`. Wrong: finding `frame-lost`. Right: no finding (the call is not in the final flow: drop the call records of ops removed with their block).")

row('A3-137', 'P3',
 "A byte-array local written wider than its symbol prints an array assignment from a cast to an array type and an undeclared name: `_axStack_14 = CONCAT31((unkuint3)param_1,0x51); axStack_14 = (xunknown1  [2])CONCAT11(param_1,param_4); return _axStack_14 + 0x51 + (int4)xStack_12;` (a 4-byte word built by byte stores at S+0 to S+3 and a halfword, read whole). The values are those of the code (the printed-C differential cannot say: the text does not compile, `cast specifies array type`), but `axStack_14` is an array that is assigned, `_axStack_14` is declared nowhere, and a reader cannot tell which bytes each statement writes. The harness made `_xStack_14` over `xStack_12` work for scalars (A3-124 fix, H_cover); an array local has no such rule. Found by the generator's own frame124 stream in the printed-C range 3,900,001 to 3,902,000 (`frame124_3900950`, 1 of 2,690 compared functions); also 1 of 700 call-free fixtures of `frame15.py` seed 31 (F15.31.408, `axStack_24 = (xunknown1  [3])CONCAT21(0,param_1);`). No wrong value found; P3 as misleading print, P2 if the array assignment is read as a statement the code makes. `frame124_3909309` (printed-C 3,908,001 to 3,910,000) is the same print with three underscore writes over `xStack_14` and `axStack_13 [2]` (`_axStack_13 = CONCAT12(0x9f,(uint2)param_3 << 8); _xStack_14 = CONCAT31(_axStack_13 >> 8,param_3); _xStack_14 = CONCAT13(param_1,_axStack_13);`) and the differential reports a `mismatch` (run 009FFEFF, text FFFFFF9F) that I could not separate from the harness's missing rule for an array under an underscore write; the base's text for the same code has no parameters at all (`func(void)`, A3-124's drop), so the head is no worse.",
 "`SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS=\"7FE0 61F3 7108 1F17 51F7 6313 7301 2360 51F7 6313 2350 51F7 6313 7304 2340 51F7 E251 6313 7307 2320 51F7 6313 7306 2340 51F7 6313 7302 2370 51F7 6313 7305 2370 51F7 6313 7303 2360 51F7 6313 2350 63F3 730F 6230 6023 63F3 730C 6232 302C 63F3 730E 6231 302C 7F20 000B 0009\"` (finding `not-compilable`, `frame124_3900950`); `g3909309.spec` and `SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS=\"7FE0 61F3 7108 1F17 51F7 6313 7303 2360 51F7 6313 7306 2360 51F7 6313 7301 2360 51F7 E29F 6313 7305 2320 51F7 6313 7307 2360 51F7 6313 7304 2340 51F7 6313 7302 2370 51F7 6313 2360 51F7 6313 7302 2360 51F7 6313 7304 6231 6023 51F7 6313 7305 6230 202A 51F7 6313 7304 6232 302C 7F20 000B 0009\"` for the mismatch. Wrong: an array assigned a cast to an array type, `_axStack_14` undeclared. Right: `xStack_14 = CONCAT...` as one scalar or a declaration for the underscore name, so the text compiles and the differential can compare it.")

row('A3-138', 'P3',
 "Frame ledger false loss, the second class (oracle; A3-136 is the first): a word written to the frame before a call that is handed the struct, and written again after the call (the `callr` shape), is split by the engine into two halfword writes, both printed before the call (`aiStack_1c[2] = -1; aiStack_1c[3] = -0x55;` for the word 0xFFFFFFAB), and the ledger reports the word as lost: 'the frame write of 4 byte(s) at 06009014 (frame offset FFFFFFE8) went unread, and the read at 00000000 reads it' (the read is the call). The halves are new COPYs the records do not hold, and the destroyed word COPY has no forwarded reader, so `fate_frame_unread` is recorded for a write whose bytes are in the text. No wrong text. Two findings of the generator's frame124 stream in the printed-C range 3,906,001 to 3,908,000 (`frame124_3907314`, `frame124_3907962`: 2 of 2,858 compared functions); the nightly would be red on a longer range.",
 "`SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_FIRST=3907314 SE_GENERATED_SEEDS=1 SaturnExplorerSh2GeneratedTests <dir>` (and 3907962). Words of 3907314: `4F22 7FE0 61F3 7108 1F17 51F7 E294 1120 51F7 E2AB 1121 51F7 E224 6313 2321 54F7 D037 400B 0009 E28D 51F7 1121 51F7 6313 6231 6023 51F7 6313 7302 6230 302C 7F20 4F26 000B 0009` with the callee at 060090C0 (a pool word `0600 90C0` after the padding). Wrong: finding `frame-lost`. Right: no finding (a split write is the same bytes: the halves should carry the word's record).")

print('\n'.join(rows))
